+## Subtopics
+
+### Gaussian Opacity Fields (GOF)
+A method that represents opacity as a continuous field, differing from discrete Gaussian splats. It is chosen when smooth opacity transitions are required without discrete primitives. See [Gaussian Opacity Fields (GOF)](5992-gaussian-opacity-fields-gof.md).
+
+### QuadBox
+A method that approximates Gaussian splats with axis-aligned bounding boxes for faster rasterization. It is chosen when performance is critical and slight accuracy trade-offs are acceptable. See [QuadBox](9231-quadbox.md).
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Reduced setup costs per Gaussian compared to standard methods, but slightly higher costs per pixel due to the Plücker coordinate calculations [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Not given in the sources [G].
-*   **Strengths:** Numerically stable; avoids matrix inversion; handles degenerate Gaussians; fast and trivial to differentiate (additions, multiplications, and one division) [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Slightly higher per-pixel cost compared to approximations, making it less ideal when primitives cover many pixels [S1].
-*   **When to Use:** Desirable when graphics primitives cover only a few pixels [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** The method incurs reduced setup costs per Gaussian compared to methods requiring matrix inversion but slightly higher costs per pixel due to the Plücker coordinate calculations [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing the transformation matrix $T'$ and Plücker coordinates, but avoids storing large intermediate matrices for inversion [S1].
+*   **Measured Performance:** The source notes that the tradeoff is desirable when graphics primitives cover only few pixels [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Numerically stable for degenerate splats (vanishing axes), no matrix inversion required, closed-form bounding box calculation [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Slightly higher per-pixel cost compared to standard rasterization [S1].
+*   **When to Use:** When rendering non-degenerate 3D Gaussians with perspectively correct ray-splat intersection, especially when numerical stability is a concern.
+*   **When Not to Use:** When per-pixel performance is the absolute highest priority and degeneracy is not a concern.
 
 ## Background
-This method was introduced in "Efficient Perspective-Correct 3D Gaussian Splatting Using Hybrid Transparency" [S1]. It builds on Weyrich et al.'s optimization of Sigg et al.'s bounding box computation [SWBG06] and extends it to non-degenerate 3D Gaussians [S1]. It also follows Radl et al. [RSP*24] for opacity-based cutoffs and contrasts with concurrent works like [YSG24, MLMP*24] which use numerically unstable or slower approaches [S1].
+This method was introduced by Radl et al. in "Efficient Perspective-Correct 3D Gaussian Splatting Using Hybrid Transparency" [S1]. It extends the perspectively correct approach of Weyrich et al. [WHA*07] and Sigg et al. [SWBG06] to work with non-degenerate 3D Gaussians, enabling the use of ray-splat intersection for rasterization-based rendering with negligible overhead [S1]. It addresses the numerical instabilities of matrix inversion-based methods like those in Kerbl et al. [KKLD23] and Huang et al. [HYC*24] (which only works for 2D Gaussians) [S1].
 
